{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551002229,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799a7bffe903YpgFMcmoKDC","part":{"partID":"prt_1d39332150024CUABw1pi4xe7q","sessionID":"ses_e2c799a7bffe903YpgFMcmoKDC","messageID":"msg_1d3931898001DKf6NTwKXcDGkd","type":"tool","id":"call_3586b606a82d4a24abb56a3d","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":300,"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-300\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n56: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n57: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n58: \n59: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n60: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n61: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n62: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n63: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n64: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n65: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n66: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n67: \n68: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n69: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n70: \n71: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n72: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n73: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n74:   chat: 300_000,\n75:   started: 900_000,\n76: });\n77: \n78: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n79: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n80: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n81: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n82: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n83: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n84: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n85: \n86: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n87: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n88: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n89: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n90:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n91: });\n92: \n93: function has(object, key) {\n94:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n95: }\n96: \n97: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n98: // Runtime argv table\n99: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n100: \n101: const RUNTIMES = {\n102:   claude: {\n103:     command: 'claude',\n104:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n105:     promptOnStdin: false,\n106:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n107:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n108:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n109:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n110:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n111:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n112:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n113:       '-p',\n114:       prompt,\n115:       '--model',\n116:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n117:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n118:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n119:       '--output-format',\n120:       'stream-json',\n121:       '--verbose',\n122:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n123:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n124:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n125:       '--permission-mode',\n126:       'bypassPermissions',\n127:     ],\n128:   },\n129:   codex: {\n130:     command: 'codex',\n131:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n132:     promptOnStdin: true,\n133:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n134:     // named after that thread id.\n135:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n136:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n137:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n138:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n139:     buildArgv: () => [\n140:       'exec',\n141:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n142:       '--json',\n143:       '--model',\n144:       CODEX_MODEL,\n145:       '-c',\n146:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n147:       '-c',\n148:       'service_tier=fast',\n149:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n150:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n151:       '-c',\n152:       'approval_policy=never',\n153:       '--sandbox',\n154:       'workspace-write',\n155:       '-',\n156:     ],\n157:   },\n158:   opencode: {\n159:     command: 'opencode',\n160:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n161:     promptOnStdin: false,\n162:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n163:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n164:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n165:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n166:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n167:       'run',\n168:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n169:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n170:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n171:       '--standalone',\n172:       '--model',\n173:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n174:       '--format',\n175:       'json',\n176:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n177:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n178:       '--auto',\n179:       prompt,\n180:     ],\n181:   },\n182:   pi: {\n183:     command: 'pi',\n184:     model: PI_MODEL,\n185:     promptOnStdin: false,\n186:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n187:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n188:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n189:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n190:       '-p',\n191:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n192:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n193:       '--offline',\n194:       '--model',\n195:       PI_MODEL,\n196:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n197:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n198:       '--session-id',\n199:       sessionId,\n200:       prompt,\n201:     ],\n202:   },\n203:   devin: {\n204:     command: 'devin',\n205:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n206:     promptOnStdin: false,\n207:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n208:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n209:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n210:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n211:       '-p',\n212:       '--model',\n213:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n214:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n215:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n216:       '--permission-mode',\n217:       'dangerous',\n218:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n219:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n220:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n221:       'false',\n222:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n223:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n224:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n225:       // arena instead.\n226:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n227:       '--',\n228:       prompt,\n229:     ],\n230:   },\n231:   dsh: {\n232:     command: 'dsh',\n233:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n234:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n235:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n236:     promptOnStdin: true,\n237:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n238:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n239:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n240:     buildArgv: () => [\n241:       '--profile',\n242:       'headless',\n243:       '--patch',\n244:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n245:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n246:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n247:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n248:       '--patch',\n249:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n250:       '--json',\n251:       '--permission-mode',\n252:       'workspace-write',\n253:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n254:       '--approval-policy',\n255:       'never',\n256:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n257:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n258:       // flag.\n259:       '-',\n260:     ],\n261:   },\n262: };\n263: \n264: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n265: // Session locator\n266: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n267: //\n268: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n269: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n270: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n271: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n272: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n273: // newest file in the store.\n274: \n275: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n276: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n277: // the id never changes within one turn.\n278: function streamLocator(field) {\n279:   let pending = '';\n280:   let captured = null;\n281:   return {\n282:     push(chunk) {\n283:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n284:       pending += chunk;\n285:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n286:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n287:       for (const line of lines) {\n288:         captured = readId(line, field);\n289:         if (captured !== null) return;\n290:       }\n291:     },\n292:     value() {\n293:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n294:       return captured;\n295:     },\n296:   };\n297: }\n298: \n299: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n300: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 301]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-300\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n56: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n57: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n58: \n59: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n60: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n61: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n62: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n63: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n64: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n65: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n66: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n67: \n68: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n69: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n70: \n71: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n72: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n73: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n74:   chat: 300_000,\n75:   started: 900_000,\n76: });\n77: \n78: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n79: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n80: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n81: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n82: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n83: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n84: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n85: \n86: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n87: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n88: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n89: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n90:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n91: });\n92: \n93: function has(object, key) {\n94:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n95: }\n96: \n97: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n98: // Runtime argv table\n99: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n100: \n101: const RUNTIMES = {\n102:   claude: {\n103:     command: 'claude',\n104:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n105:     promptOnStdin: false,\n106:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n107:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n108:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n109:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n110:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n111:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n112:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n113:       '-p',\n114:       prompt,\n115:       '--model',\n116:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n117:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n118:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n119:       '--output-format',\n120:       'stream-json',\n121:       '--verbose',\n122:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n123:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n124:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n125:       '--permission-mode',\n126:       'bypassPermissions',\n127:     ],\n128:   },\n129:   codex: {\n130:     command: 'codex',\n131:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n132:     promptOnStdin: true,\n133:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n134:     // named after that thread id.\n135:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n136:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n137:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n138:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n139:     buildArgv: () => [\n140:       'exec',\n141:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n142:       '--json',\n143:       '--model',\n144:       CODEX_MODEL,\n145:       '-c',\n146:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n147:       '-c',\n148:       'service_tier=fast',\n149:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n150:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n151:       '-c',\n152:       'approval_policy=never',\n153:       '--sandbox',\n154:       'workspace-write',\n155:       '-',\n156:     ],\n157:   },\n158:   opencode: {\n159:     command: 'opencode',\n160:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n161:     promptOnStdin: false,\n162:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n163:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n164:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n165:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n166:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n167:       'run',\n168:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n169:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n170:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n171:       '--standalone',\n172:       '--model',\n173:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n174:       '--format',\n175:       'json',\n176:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n177:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n178:       '--auto',\n179:       prompt,\n180:     ],\n181:   },\n182:   pi: {\n183:     command: 'pi',\n184:     model: PI_MODEL,\n185:     promptOnStdin: false,\n186:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n187:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n188:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n189:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n190:       '-p',\n191:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n192:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n193:       '--offline',\n194:       '--model',\n195:       PI_MODEL,\n196:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n197:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n198:       '--session-id',\n199:       sessionId,\n200:       prompt,\n201:     ],\n202:   },\n203:   devin: {\n204:     command: 'devin',\n205:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n206:     promptOnStdin: false,\n207:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n208:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n209:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n210:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n211:       '-p',\n212:       '--model',\n213:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n214:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n215:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n216:       '--permission-mode',\n217:       'dangerous',\n218:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n219:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n220:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n221:       'false',\n222:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n223:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n224:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n225:       // arena instead.\n226:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n227:       '--',\n228:       prompt,\n229:     ],\n230:   },\n231:   dsh: {\n232:     command: 'dsh',\n233:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n234:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n235:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n236:     promptOnStdin: true,\n237:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n238:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n239:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n240:     buildArgv: () => [\n241:       '--profile',\n242:       'headless',\n243:       '--patch',\n244:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n245:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n246:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n247:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n248:       '--patch',\n249:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n250:       '--json',\n251:       '--permission-mode',\n252:       'workspace-write',\n253:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n254:       '--approval-policy',\n255:       'never',\n256:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n257:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n258:       // flag.\n259:       '-',\n260:     ],\n261:   },\n262: };\n263: \n264: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n265: // Session locator\n266: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n267: //\n268: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n269: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n270: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n271: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n272: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n273: // newest file in the store.\n274: \n275: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n276: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n277: // the id never changes within one turn.\n278: function streamLocator(field) {\n279:   let pending = '';\n280:   let captured = null;\n281:   return {\n282:     push(chunk) {\n283:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n284:       pending += chunk;\n285:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n286:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n287:       for (const line of lines) {\n288:         captured = readId(line, field);\n289:         if (captured !== null) return;\n290:       }\n291:     },\n292:     value() {\n293:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n294:       return captured;\n295:     },\n296:   };\n297: }\n298: \n299: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n300: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 301]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\":300,\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794551001621,"end":1794551002229}}}}
